ricardo hausmann is a venezuelan  economist teaching economic development  at harvard kennedy school he's been  speaking to our editor-in-chief manuela  caspar claridge at the world economic  forum meeting in davos  ricardo the german chancellor has asked  on oil producing countries to produce  more oil to dispense of russian oil how  realistic is this from your point of  view i think there's an interesting  agreement to be made  we need to separate opec from russia as  you know russia joined opec in december  2016 in a structure called opec plus  i think the basis of the bargain is the  following  the west is going to make sure that  russia gets no access to financial  markets that they get no access to oil  technology and that they get no access  to oil markets that's going to reduce  the ability for russia to invest in oil  and to maintain production  this is going to be good for  decarbonization so because what matters  for decarbonization is the reduction in  global oil production  but we need to decarbonize in a way that  assures energy security and energy  affordability  so we need opec to guarantee energy  security in a process in which the  market share of russia is going to  shrink and that's in the interest of the  saudis the emiratis and the other oil  producers so i think that that deal  should be on the table and i think they  should sit down to negotiate but how  long could this take i mean this would  take quite a while i guess  we're not in a hurry this is important  for the long term in the short term  the discussion in europe has been  completely derailed by the idea of  becoming independent from russia and to  embargo russian oil  this  may be good for europe in the medium  term to assure european security but  right now there's a war going on and you  want to harm russia so the way to harm  russia is not to embargo russian oil it  is to tax russian oil  today tomorrow immediately tax russian  oil that will imply that russian oil  will have to complete peat with saudi  oil in the european market and they will  have to be sold at the same price  controlling for quality  at the same price but the only problem  is that the russian price includes a tax  and the saudi price doesn't so saudi oil  would be cheaper obviously no so the  russian oil will have to go down in  price russia will have to give bigger  discounts we already see that the  discount between euros oil the russian  oil and and and brent the standard oil  the benchmark  used to be three dollars now it's thirty  dollars let's make it into sixty dollars  or seventy dollars and that's the way to  hurt poutine now  let's talk about in energy security in  european geosecurity uh you know in  december if you want but right now  there's a war going on in russia in in  ukraine and we need to reduce the cash  flow of mr putin and we do that with  taxing russian oil and then you can use  that money to rebuild ukraine  to subsidize consumers  to  help  ukrainian refugees whatever but ricardo  doesn't want change one depends then  dependency to the other  i think that in the long run  we need to decarbonize but we're not  going to decarbonize in  in a year that's why the target is 2050.  we need to assure that we get to 2015 at  zero but in in the meantime we need to  assure energy security and energy  affordability the world the atmosphere  doesn't care whose oil got burned  right but we care whose oil got burned  because  there are some countries that if they  sell oil and make a lot of money they  use that to attack other countries that  is a very expensive oil because you have  to count the cost of oil and your cost  of defense and your cost of  administering refugees and your cost of  helping the other country so russian oil  is the most expensive oil there is we  need to get rid of it but we will get  rid of it gradually  in the meantime we will tax it  cardio houseman from harvard kennedy  school speaking to manuela casper  claridge there
 

